{"name":"Shark Fin","hex":"#969795","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"shark fin","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An upward curled felt side lap or end lap.","Used other than figuratively or idiomatically: the fin of a shark, specifically the dorsal, pectoral, or caudal fin.."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/shark_fin"}],"entries":[{"title":"Shark","blocks":[{"text":"Sharks are a group of elasmobranch cartilaginous fishes characterized by a ribless endoskeleton, dermal denticles, five to seven gill slits on each side, and pectoral fins that are not fused to the head. Modern sharks are classified within the division Selachii and are the sister group to the Batomorphi. Some sources extend the term \"shark\" as an informal category including extinct members of Chondrichthyes with a shark-like morphology, such as hybodonts. Shark-like chondrichthyans such as Cladoselache and Doliodus first appeared in the Devonian Period, though some fossilized chondrichthyan-like scales are as old as the Late Ordovician. The earliest confirmed modern sharks (Selachii) are known from the Early Jurassic around 200 million years ago, with the oldest known member being Agaleus, though records of true sharks may extend back as far as the Permian."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shark","wikidata":"Q7372"},{"title":"Shark fin soup","blocks":[{"text":"Shark fin soup is a soup or stewed dish served in parts of China, Taiwan, and Southeast Asia. The shark fins provide texture, while the taste comes from the other ingredients. As one of the four sea delicacies, it is commonly served at special occasions such as weddings and banquets, or as a luxury item."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Chinese_cuisine-Shark_fin_soup-04.jpg/330px-Chinese_cuisine-Shark_fin_soup-04.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shark_fin_soup","wikidata":"Q26081"},{"title":"Fin","blocks":[{"text":"A fin is a thin appendage or component attached to a larger body or structure. Fins typically function as foils that produce lift or thrust, or provide the ability to steer or stabilize motion while traveling in water, air, or other fluids. Fins are also used to increase surface areas for heat transfer purposes, or simply as ornamentation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Trailing_edge_NACA_0012.svg/330px-Trailing_edge_NACA_0012.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fin","wikidata":"Q778612"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Corl0207_%2828225976491%29.jpg/500px-Corl0207_%2828225976491%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Corl0207_%2828225976491%29.jpg/1280px-Corl0207_%2828225976491%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Corl0207_%2828225976491%29.jpg/500px-Corl0207_%2828225976491%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACorl0207_(28225976491).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Fossiler_Haifischzahn_-_Gr%C3%B6%C3%9Fe_%C3%BCber_9_cm_-_mit_Krone_-_Schulter_-_Wurzel_und_Wurzellappen.jpg/500px-Fossiler_Haifischzahn_-_Gr%C3%B6%C3%9Fe_%C3%BCber_9_cm_-_mit_Krone_-_Schulter_-_Wurzel_und_Wurzellappen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Fossiler_Haifischzahn_-_Gr%C3%B6%C3%9Fe_%C3%BCber_9_cm_-_mit_Krone_-_Schulter_-_Wurzel_und_Wurzellappen.jpg/1280px-Fossiler_Haifischzahn_-_Gr%C3%B6%C3%9Fe_%C3%BCber_9_cm_-_mit_Krone_-_Schulter_-_Wurzel_und_Wurzellappen.j